1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,146
It's the fourth quarter with 2
minutes on the clock.

2
00:00:03,146 --> 00:00:05,732
You get an open look at the
three-point line.

3
00:00:06,082 --> 00:00:09,645
You rise up to shoot, but your legs
feel like lead pipes.

4
00:00:09,995 --> 00:00:12,470
The ball clangs flat off the
front of the rim.

5
00:00:12,819 --> 00:00:17,238
On the next possession, you're bent over
with your hands on your knees,

6
00:00:17,238 --> 00:00:20,798
watching an opponent blow past you
for a transition layup.

7
00:00:21,148 --> 00:00:23,815
You don't lack heart, and you
don't lack skill.

8
00:00:23,815 --> 00:00:26,204
You are simply running on an
empty battery.

9
00:00:26,554 --> 00:00:31,480
Today: how to condition your body for 40
minutes of full-court basketball,

10
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:35,749
so you finish the fourth quarter as
explosive as the opening tip.

11
00:00:36,349 --> 00:00:39,634
Basketball fatigue is different
from any other sport.

12
00:00:39,984 --> 00:00:42,607
A marathon runner can run 26 miles,

13
00:00:42,607 --> 00:00:47,707
but they will be gasping for air after 5
minutes of pickup basketball.

14
00:00:48,057 --> 00:00:54,244
That's because basketball demands over 900 rapid
changes of speed and direction in a

15
00:00:54,244 --> 00:00:55,118
single game.

16
00:00:55,468 --> 00:00:58,881
To dominate all 4 quarters, you
need four pillars.

17
00:00:59,231 --> 00:01:01,964
Pillar 1: the three energy engines.

18
00:01:01,964 --> 00:01:04,621
Pillar 2: repeated sprint ability.

19
00:01:04,621 --> 00:01:07,051
Pillar 3: deceleration economy.

20
00:01:07,051 --> 00:01:09,556
And Pillar 4: dead-ball recovery.

21
00:01:09,907 --> 00:01:14,088
Master these, and you will outlast
every defender on the floor.

22
00:01:14,438 --> 00:01:18,769
Your body relies on three distinct
energy engines during a game.

23
00:01:19,119 --> 00:01:24,047
Engine 1 is your ATP-PC system, your
explosive rocket fuel.

24
00:01:24,397 --> 00:01:29,522
It powers your fast-break sprints, contest
jumps, and crossover drives,

25
00:01:29,522 --> 00:01:32,013
which last between 2 and 4 seconds.

26
00:01:32,363 --> 00:01:36,071
Engine 2 is glycolysis, which
kicks in during frantic,

27
00:01:36,071 --> 00:01:39,104
back-and-forth possessions
without a whistle.

28
00:01:39,454 --> 00:01:42,201
Glycolysis burns muscle sugars fast,

29
00:01:42,201 --> 00:01:46,729
flooding your bloodstream with lactic
acid and hydrogen ions.

30
00:01:47,079 --> 00:01:52,142
That acid buildup is why your thighs burn and
your jump height drops late in the

31
00:01:52,142 --> 00:01:52,455
half.

32
00:01:52,805 --> 00:01:55,183
Engine 3 is your aerobic engine.

33
00:01:55,183 --> 00:01:58,138
And here is the secret most hoopers miss.

34
00:01:58,488 --> 00:02:04,504
Phosphocreatine, your explosive rocket fuel,
cannot recharge without oxygen.

35
00:02:04,854 --> 00:02:10,221
Your aerobic engine is the only system in your
body that refills your explosive

36
00:02:10,221 --> 00:02:13,643
battery during whistles, free
throws, and timeouts.

37
00:02:13,993 --> 00:02:16,484
Think of a smartphone fast-charger.

38
00:02:16,484 --> 00:02:20,982
The explosive battery drains in
seconds when running heavy apps.

39
00:02:20,982 --> 00:02:24,788
Your aerobic engine is the
high-wattage charging cable.

40
00:02:25,138 --> 00:02:30,814
If your aerobic charger is weak, you start
every fourth-quarter possession with a

41
00:02:30,814 --> 00:02:31,645
20% battery.

42
00:02:31,995 --> 00:02:35,131
To build this engine, use
alactic court intervals.

43
00:02:35,481 --> 00:02:39,273
Sprint from baseline to the
half-court line and back,

44
00:02:39,273 --> 00:02:42,222
about 28 meters all-out in 5 to 6 seconds.

45
00:02:42,572 --> 00:02:47,031
Rest passively for 30 seconds to allow
partial battery recharge.

46
00:02:47,381 --> 00:02:51,885
Perform 2 sets of 5 reps, resting 3
minutes between sets.

47
00:02:51,885 --> 00:02:58,176
Beginners start with 1 set of 4 to 5 reps in
week 1 before progressing to 2 sets.

48
00:02:58,525 --> 00:03:00,065
The common mistake?

49
00:03:00,065 --> 00:03:02,375
Running endless slow 5k jogs.

50
00:03:02,375 --> 00:03:06,456
Long, slow jogging trains
slow-twitch muscle fibers,

51
00:03:06,456 --> 00:03:10,152
doing nothing to recharge
explosive court speed.

52
00:03:10,502 --> 00:03:16,672
In an average 40-minute game, a basketball
player executes over 100 high-intensity

53
00:03:16,672 --> 00:03:17,713
sprint bursts.

54
00:03:18,063 --> 00:03:23,268
That means you explode into a sprint roughly
every 21 seconds of live play.

55
00:03:23,618 --> 00:03:29,650
Having a high VO2 max is useless if your body
cannot recover between max-effort

56
00:03:29,650 --> 00:03:30,253
sprints.

57
00:03:30,603 --> 00:03:35,062
Sports scientists call this Repeated
Sprint Ability, or RSA.

58
00:03:35,411 --> 00:03:40,254
Think of a high-performance drag racer paired
with a massive cooling radiator.

59
00:03:40,604 --> 00:03:47,044
The engine revs to 8,000 RPM for 3 seconds,
dumps heat during a 20-second pit stop,

60
00:03:47,044 --> 00:03:50,417
and fires up again without
blowing a gasket.

61
00:03:50,767 --> 00:03:56,320
Picture a wing defender fighting through a
double screen, contesting a corner three,

62
00:03:56,320 --> 00:04:00,175
and immediately sprinting 28 meters
downcourt on the break.

63
00:04:00,525 --> 00:04:06,690
If your RSA is weak, your power output drops on
every single sprint after halftime.

64
00:04:07,041 --> 00:04:12,657
Here is a game-changing lab finding: passive
rest between sprints recharges your

65
00:04:12,657 --> 00:04:15,916
power significantly better
than active jogging.

66
00:04:16,265 --> 00:04:21,765
Jogging between sprints consumes the exact
oxygen your mitochondria need to

67
00:04:21,765 --> 00:04:23,646
replenish phosphocreatine.

68
00:04:23,997 --> 00:04:26,591
When play stops, stand or walk slowly.

69
00:04:26,591 --> 00:04:28,520
Never jog back to the huddle.

70
00:04:28,869 --> 00:04:33,093
To train RSA, use 15-on,
15-off court shuttles.

71
00:04:33,443 --> 00:04:37,716
Sprint court shuttles at 90%
intensity for 15 seconds,

72
00:04:37,716 --> 00:04:40,824
followed by 15 seconds of standing rest.

73
00:04:41,175 --> 00:04:46,146
Run two 6-minute blocks, resting 3 minutes
between blocks, once per week.

74
00:04:46,495 --> 00:04:48,573
The mistake within the fix?

75
00:04:48,573 --> 00:04:52,283
Jogging through conditioning
drills at 70% speed.

76
00:04:52,283 --> 00:04:58,442
If you don't sprint at 100% effort, your nervous
system never adapts to game speed.

77
00:04:58,792 --> 00:05:02,845
Every time you sprint on a basketball
court, you have to stop.

78
00:05:03,195 --> 00:05:08,658
Studies show that basketball players perform
as many violent decelerations as

79
00:05:08,658 --> 00:05:09,638
accelerations.

80
00:05:09,988 --> 00:05:16,059
Stopping your momentum creates ground impact
forces exceeding 4 to 6 times your body

81
00:05:16,059 --> 00:05:16,559
weight.

82
00:05:16,909 --> 00:05:21,042
Untrained players brake with their knees
straight and chests upright,

83
00:05:21,042 --> 00:05:23,522
slamming force directly into their joints.

84
00:05:23,872 --> 00:05:29,823
That is why 32% of elite basketball players
suffer from patellar tendinopathy,

85
00:05:29,823 --> 00:05:31,104
or jumper's knee.

86
00:05:31,454 --> 00:05:33,854
Think of the brake pads on a race car.

87
00:05:33,854 --> 00:05:39,023
Jamming the brakes with poor technique overheats
the pads and burns through kinetic

88
00:05:39,023 --> 00:05:39,454
energy.

89
00:05:39,804 --> 00:05:43,687
Smooth braking turns kinetic force
into elastic rebound.

90
00:05:44,037 --> 00:05:46,761
Picture a ball-handler
attacking the paint.

91
00:05:46,761 --> 00:05:50,970
If they brake with high, stiff hips,
they stumble and lose the ball.

92
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:54,717
If they sink their hips into a
compact 2-step stop,

93
00:05:54,717 --> 00:05:57,656
they rise smoothly into a
balanced jump shot.

94
00:05:58,006 --> 00:06:00,715
The fix: master the low-hip 2-step brake.

95
00:06:01,065 --> 00:06:03,905
Drop your hips low on your
penultimate step,

96
00:06:03,905 --> 00:06:08,702
keeping a positive shin angle to absorb
force through your glutes and quads.

97
00:06:09,053 --> 00:06:15,847
To bulletproof your knees, perform isometric
Spanish squats: wrap a heavy resistance

98
00:06:15,847 --> 00:06:22,322
band behind your knees, sit back into a
45-degree squat, and hold for 45 seconds.

99
00:06:22,672 --> 00:06:29,819
Do 3 sets of 45-second holds before practice,
resting 60 to 90 seconds between sets.

100
00:06:30,169 --> 00:06:31,878
The mistake within the fix?

101
00:06:31,878 --> 00:06:34,930
Stomping the lead heel into the
hardwood to stop.

102
00:06:34,930 --> 00:06:38,532
Always brake through the ball of
your foot with bent knees.

103
00:06:38,881 --> 00:06:45,187
Here is an eye-opening statistic: live play
accounts for less than 50% of total game

104
00:06:45,187 --> 00:06:45,558
time.

105
00:06:45,909 --> 00:06:49,493
The other half of the game is spent
at the free-throw line,

106
00:06:49,493 --> 00:06:53,077
waiting for out-of-bounds whistles,
and sitting in timeouts.

107
00:06:53,427 --> 00:06:57,907
Whoever recovers fastest during those
stoppages wins the fourth quarter.

108
00:06:58,257 --> 00:07:04,823
In sports science trials, shooting jump shots or
free throws with a heart rate over

109
00:07:04,823 --> 00:07:08,497
80% of max drops shooting
accuracy by over 20%.

110
00:07:08,847 --> 00:07:13,540
That explains why tired players miss
front-rim free throws late in games.

111
00:07:13,890 --> 00:07:16,258
The fix is two dead-ball protocols.

112
00:07:16,608 --> 00:07:19,360
First: the hands-on-knees
recovery posture.

113
00:07:19,710 --> 00:07:24,689
For generations, old-school coaches ordered
players to never put their hands on

114
00:07:24,689 --> 00:07:27,241
their knees, claiming it showed weakness.

115
00:07:27,591 --> 00:07:29,746
Science proved those coaches wrong.

116
00:07:30,095 --> 00:07:35,364
A landmark study showed that resting your
hands on your knees significantly

117
00:07:35,364 --> 00:07:40,981
accelerates heart rate recovery and increases
oxygen intake compared to standing

118
00:07:40,981 --> 00:07:42,298
with hands on head.

119
00:07:42,648 --> 00:07:48,237
Bending forward stabilizes your ribcage and
optimizes diaphragmatic breathing.

120
00:07:48,587 --> 00:07:50,635
Second: the physiological sigh.

121
00:07:50,985 --> 00:07:54,634
When standing at the free-throw
line or during a stoppage,

122
00:07:54,634 --> 00:07:58,715
take two quick sniffs in through your
nose, followed by one long,

123
00:07:58,715 --> 00:08:00,756
slow sigh out through your mouth.

124
00:08:01,106 --> 00:08:05,362
This double-inhale pops open
collapsed air sacs in your lungs,

125
00:08:05,362 --> 00:08:09,618
immediately dumping carbon dioxide and
slowing your heart rate.

126
00:08:09,968 --> 00:08:11,706
The mistake within the fix?

127
00:08:11,706 --> 00:08:15,243
Frantically pacing around the
court between free throws,

128
00:08:15,243 --> 00:08:17,477
which keeps your adrenaline spiking.

129
00:08:17,827 --> 00:08:21,570
Indoor basketball courts trap
heat and humidity,

130
00:08:21,570 --> 00:08:24,549
producing sweat rates between 1 and 1.

131
00:08:24,549 --> 00:08:25,848
5 liters per hour.

132
00:08:26,199 --> 00:08:31,934
Clinical basketball studies prove that losing
just 2% of your body weight in sweat

133
00:08:31,934 --> 00:08:37,186
causes a steep, progressive decline in
shooting accuracy and reaction speed.

134
00:08:37,535 --> 00:08:43,380
Drink 500 milliliters of water with sodium
electrolytes 2 hours before tip-off.

135
00:08:43,731 --> 00:08:49,107
During the game, take 3 to 4 swallows of fluid
at every substitution and timeout.

136
00:08:49,457 --> 00:08:55,068
If you wait until you feel thirsty, you are
already dehydrated by 1 to 2%.

137
00:08:55,417 --> 00:09:00,957
High-intensity basketball conditioning places
immense demands on your Achilles

138
00:09:00,957 --> 00:09:02,990
tendons and patellar tendons.

139
00:09:03,341 --> 00:09:09,445
Always complete an 8 to 10 minute dynamic
warm-up: high knees, defensive shuffles,

140
00:09:09,445 --> 00:09:13,048
karaoke steps, and progressive
acceleration runs.

141
00:09:13,397 --> 00:09:16,085
Build your conditioning volume gradually.

142
00:09:16,085 --> 00:09:20,437
Start with one conditioning session per
week before adding a second.

143
00:09:20,787 --> 00:09:26,408
If you experience sharp, localized pain at the
base of your kneecap or behind your

144
00:09:26,408 --> 00:09:30,878
heel that lingers the next morning,
stop explosive jumping drills.

145
00:09:31,228 --> 00:09:36,007
See a sports physiotherapist to assess
tendon load before you sprint again.

146
00:09:36,357 --> 00:09:40,410
Here is a simple conditioning
structure for basketball players.

147
00:09:40,760 --> 00:09:45,244
If you have team practice or games on
Tuesday, Thursday, and Saturday,

148
00:09:45,244 --> 00:09:47,075
fit conditioning around them.

149
00:09:47,425 --> 00:09:51,082
Monday: dynamic warm-up,
isometric Spanish squats,

150
00:09:51,082 --> 00:09:54,166
followed by alactic court
sprint intervals.

151
00:09:54,516 --> 00:10:00,893
Wednesday: dynamic warm-up, 15-on, 15-off
Repeated Sprint Ability shuttles for two

152
00:10:00,893 --> 00:10:05,119
6-minute blocks, followed by an
active flush on Friday.

153
00:10:05,469 --> 00:10:11,114
During games: practice the hands-on-knees
posture and physiological sighs at every

154
00:10:11,114 --> 00:10:12,338
single free throw.

155
00:10:12,688 --> 00:10:16,958
Within 3 to 4 weeks, your aerobic
recharge rate will surge,

156
00:10:16,958 --> 00:10:20,517
keeping your legs fresh and
bouncy all 40 minutes.

157
00:10:20,867 --> 00:10:24,006
Next time you step onto the court
in the fourth quarter,

158
00:10:24,006 --> 00:10:25,603
don't let your legs fail you.

159
00:10:25,953 --> 00:10:30,498
Charge your aerobic battery, stop on your
hips, breathe smart at the line,

160
00:10:30,498 --> 00:10:32,801
and knock down that game-winning shot.
